<h1>Services</h1>
<p class="lede">Four ways to work together, depending on what you need and where you're at.</p>

<p>Every project is quoted based on its complexity, scope, and estimated time — there's no public price list, because no two sites are the same.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <p>I combine manual auditing with real screen readers — NVDA, JAWS, Windows Narrator, TalkBack on Android — with automated tools like axe, using an AI-assisted workflow because axe's own interface isn't accessible with a screen reader. Automation gives a first pass and a way to cross-check what I've already found by hand; it never replaces manual testing.</p>
  <p>I work from WCAG 2.2 as the baseline, aligned with EN 301 549 and the European Accessibility Act (EAA), and when a client needs it, also with ADA and Section 508 in the United States.</p>
  <p>I don't only audit websites: also Android apps, PDF and EPUB documents, desktop software, browser extensions — any digital product.</p>
  <p>Reports adapt to who's going to read them: a full Word or PDF document with a criteria table, screenshots, and examples; an Excel breakdown if full technical detail is needed; or a plain-language summary for people who don't work in accessibility. Beyond flagging the problem, I propose concrete fixes. The communication channel stays open after delivery for follow-up questions, especially on the full package.</p>

  <h2>Express audit</h2>
  <p>An exploratory review of the home page and a selection of key links, with direct observations and comments — for when you need to know fast where your site stands before committing to a bigger project. It doesn't include the full page-by-page report: that's what the site audit adds.</p>
  <div class="placeholder">Still to confirm: the specific turnaround for this service. What you gave me (2 weeks to a month) seems to match the full package, not the express audit — let me know the real turnaround for this one.</div>

  <h2>Site audit</h2>
  <p>Evaluated against WCAG level AA as the floor, with specific suggestions for achievable AAA criteria where it makes sense. A full report — every subpage, every link — with a criteria table, examples, and screenshots. Closes with a call to go through the findings with whoever you want on your team: I handle the functional and screen-reader side, and my teammate handles the visual review.</p>
  <div class="placeholder">Still to confirm: whether the scope is always the whole site or a negotiated set of representative pages, and the typical turnaround.</div>

  <h2>Full package</h2>
  <p>Adds to the site audit: follow-up after delivery, drafting the accessibility statement, and a discount on a re-test once changes are made. If you need it, I also assess and quote remediation — I can fix the HTML and ARIA issues I find directly, not just point them out.</p>
  <p>Includes a live audit session, 30 minutes to an hour and a half depending on how much there is to show and discuss. The full written report is always included, regardless of the live session. The recording stays private on a YouTube channel, and is only published on the site if the client explicitly authorizes it.</p>

  <h2>Corporate workshop</h2>
  <p>In person in Argentina, remote for the rest of the Spanish-speaking countries, adapted to each team's needs.</p>
  <div class="placeholder">Still pending: who it's aimed at (design, development, both), duration and group size, whether it's theoretical, practical, or a mix, and what language it's delivered in.</div>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>How we work together</h2>
  <p>I work with individual clients and with agencies, with a preference for direct clients. A 50% deposit is due once the contract is signed — in writing or after a video call — with the rest due on delivery. I accept PayPal and bank transfer from Europe, Argentina, or the US. Quotes are in euros for Spain and dollars for the rest of the Americas, with special pricing for Argentina.</p>
</div>

